{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Measurable-version issues may be harder than they look; common domination may be too restrictive for the most interesting deterministic or singular classes.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurability audit","functional analysis"],"summary":"Make the dominated-prefix infinite-class theorem referee-ready: for fixed finite T and finite G, assume all prefix laws are dominated by sigma-finite measures lambda_t, reduce randomized policies to public-prefix kernels q_t(h) in Delta(G), prove weak-* compactness of the feasible kernel set, weak-* continuity of reward coordinates, and product closedness of the attainable reward set.","title":"Referee-Proof Dominated No-Gap Theorem","why_promising":"This would turn the solver's main finite-prior characterization into a real infinite-class theorem under checkable hypotheses, removing the largest formal gap in the current 'maybe solved' status."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"May collapse into abstract compactness assumptions that are hard to verify in market instances; nondominated deterministic classes may genuinely have gaps.","suggested_lenses":["generalization","counterexample search","topology of policies"],"summary":"Search for no-gap hypotheses beyond common domination, such as compact parametric classes with continuity of theta -> E_theta rewards for every prefix kernel, tight dominated approximations, or Feller-style conditional laws. In parallel, try to build a natural nondominated market class where finite-subclass Bayes gaps miss the full minimax value.","title":"Natural Infinite-Class No-Gap Conditions","why_promising":"Common domination is sufficient but narrow. A more natural compactness criterion would make the value characterization useful for realistic infinite classes, while a clean counterexample would clarify the exact boundary."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Repeated hidden blocks can fail because the best fixed comparator becomes the Bayes action, or because one reveal identifies a global type too early.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Fano/Le Cam","counterexample search"],"summary":"Develop a multi-scale hidden-terminal family that accumulates Bayes gap across many unresolved scales while preserving a fixed-comparator advantage. Use independent local hidden bits or a packing of revenue curves, and compute the least-favorable finite-grid Psi value to check whether the construction can match the power-tail upper exponent.","title":"Multi-Scale Bayes-Gap Lower Rates","why_promising":"The q-scaled one-block atom is too weak for power tails. A successful multi-scale construction is the main missing ingredient for matching lower rates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The claimed Omega(sqrt(T H)) behavior may be false in this threshold-reward geometry, or may require a comparator class not embeddable in delayed market making.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","statistical lower bounds","finite-grid computation"],"summary":"Investigate the solver's batched terminal-sample idea: H repeated quote decisions before one delayed sample from one of two close terminal distributions, repeated over batches. Prove or refute a bounded-delay lower bound of order Omega(sqrt(T H)) and then test a q-sparse variant such as Omega(min{T q, sqrt(T H q)}).","title":"Batched Terminal-Sample Lower Bound","why_promising":"If correct, this could connect delayed-feedback minimax lower bounds to the market model and provide the missing rate lower-bound mechanism."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The market's one-dimensional threshold reward curves may be too structured to embed arbitrary delayed expert lower bounds; the lower bound may lose log K or H factors.","suggested_lenses":["embedding","lower-bound transfer","finite experts"],"summary":"Ask whether standard delayed full-information expert lower bounds can be embedded into delayed market making on a finite quote grid. Construct price distributions whose grid reward vectors approximate hard expert instances, or prove an obstruction from monotonic threshold structure.","title":"Embed Delayed Expert Lower Bounds","why_promising":"The upper bound contains the delayed-experts term sqrt(T H log K). Showing this term is unavoidable, even for a finite grid, would separate algorithmic delay cost from truncation-tail cost."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"May just restate the minimax value in different notation; the challenge is to find a tail-like object that is simpler than Psi but still sharp.","suggested_lenses":["characterization","proof strategy","rate extraction"],"summary":"Define and test a predictable value-tail modulus: compare the full finite-grid Bayes value Psi_G(pi) with an H-truncated version where M_t only looks H steps ahead. Determine whether sup_pi [Psi_G(pi)-Psi_G^H(pi)] gives a necessary and sufficient truncation term, and whether it recovers the delta upper bound as a corollary.","title":"Predictable Value-Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It may bridge the gap between scalar truncation tails and the information-value characterization, preserving predictability and indistinguishability instead of raw hitting delays."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Could be outside the main exogenous horizon-aware model; standard doubling or a carefully randomized anytime policy may defeat the obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","formalization","horizon-free algorithms"],"summary":"Formalize the horizon-free known-law obstruction suggested by super-growing deterministic blocks with two possible deadlines. Prove whether every single anytime policy has linear regret along one deadline subsequence, or find an anytime construction that avoids the two-deadline tradeoff.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware. Settling the anytime variant would prevent overclaiming and may reveal a genuinely different necessary condition."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Action-dependent models may require an entirely different comparator definition; without a clean causal protocol the question can become ill-posed.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","causal counterfactuals","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit the action-dependent price regime. Either define a causal protocol in which comparator and learner rewards are evaluated on well-specified counterfactual paths, or build a minimal example showing that the exogenous delayed-experts reduction fails because unplayed quotes would have changed future prices.","title":"Action-Dependent Market Boundary","why_promising":"All accepted upper and value-characterization results rely on public exogenous prices. A precise separation would keep the final theorem honest and identify whether a separate theory is needed."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
